--- IR --->
// the one thing a declaration's position still decides: a class constructor's `$this` carries the heap
// allocation in its *initializer*, so the slot may be seated early but the allocation may not move - and
// the field write has to follow it rather than store through a handle nothing allocated yet
//
// `@__eco_alloc` rather than `@malloc` because the corpus compiles with --track-allocations, which routes
// every allocation in the language through the one counting seam. an untracked build calls `@malloc` here
// directly, and that difference is exactly why the flag is uniform across the corpus rather than a setting
CHECK: define ptr @_Counter
CHECK: %this = alloca ptr
CHECK-NOT: %value_ptr = getelementptr
CHECK: call ptr @__eco_alloc
CHECK: store ptr %obj, ptr %this
CHECK: %value_ptr = getelementptr
// every slot in the function sits in `entry`, ahead of the first store - including the loop body's `%p`,
// which is the whole point. an alloca is an instruction, so one emitted inside the loop would run once per
// turn and grow the stack, and mem2reg would not promote it because it is not in the entry block
CHECK: define i32 @_sum_pairs
CHECK: %i = alloca i32
CHECK: %acc = alloca i32
CHECK: %p = alloca %Pair
CHECK: store i32 0, ptr %i
CHECK: store i32 0, ptr %acc
// and the loop body allocates nothing at all
CHECK: body:
CHECK-NOT: alloca
CHECK: br label %loop
// **the split.** the same local with no initializer: one slot up in `entry`, but the zeroing store down in
// the block the back edge re-enters. hoisted with the alloca it would run once, and the second turn would
// read back whatever the first left in those bytes - the `0` in the output above is that assertion
CHECK: define i32 @_reset_each_turn
CHECK: %p = alloca %Pair
CHECK: br label %loop
CHECK: body:
CHECK: store %Pair zeroinitializer, ptr %p
CHECK-NOT: alloca
CHECK: br label %loop
// a declaration the flow never reaches is seated too. before the hoist it was never visited at all
CHECK: define i32 @_unreachable_decl
CHECK: %never = alloca %Pair
